<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hariphunchai</h1><p class="st-meta">629 CE – 1292 CE (approx.) · capital Lamphun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mon Buddhist kingdom in northern Thailand, founded according to legend by Queen Chamadevi, which survived until Mangrai of Lan Na took it in 1292.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Northern Thai chronicles say that the hermit founders of Hariphunchai at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamadevi">Chamadevi</a>, a princess of Lavo, to rule the new city, traditionally in the 7th century. She brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> Buddhist monks and craftsmen from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a> world, and one of her twin sons founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-lampang">Lampang</a> as a second centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hariphunchai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a>-speaking Buddhist kingdom that kept <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a> traditions alive long after they faded in the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-mon">Old Mon</a> inscriptions from the 12th and 13th centuries record its kings' merit-making, and King Adityaraja is credited with building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-that-hariphunchai">Wat Phra That Hariphunchai</a> stupa. It fought repeated wars with Lavo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1292 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ngoenyang">Ngoenyang</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> through a ruse and incorporated it into his new kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a>. Hariphunchai's Buddhist learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> script shaped the culture of Lan Na, and Lamphun's great stupa remains a major pilgrimage site.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hariphunchai.jpg" alt="Hariphunchai" width="320" height="295"><figcaption>Image: Tanakorn Srichaisuphakit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mainland_Southeast_Asia_1100_CE_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>629 CE – 1292 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lamphun</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chamadevi, Adityaraja, Yiba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Haripunjaya, Hariphunchai Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=960&amp;focus=hariphunchai" aria-label="Hariphunchai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7362 1488 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hariphunchai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7682 1744L7751 1883M7682 1744L7697 1683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7751 1883h0M7697 1683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7682 1744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7682 1744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lamphun. <a href="/atlas?year=960&amp;focus=hariphunchai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
